SUPABASE_URL==http://localhost:8000/telemetry/events
https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/tlfllnfwynaykxglsqhc
Great! Now that you have your API key, we need to store it securely so your Prefect pipeline can use it to talk to your database.

Since you are building a backend pipeline, it's a good idea to also grab your Secret key (the one starting with sb_secret_) right below the publishable key on that page. It ensures your Python script has the necessary permissions to read all raw data and write to the reporting tables without being blocked by Row Level Security (RLS).

Here are the exact steps to connect your workspace to Supabase:

1. Find Your Project URL
In addition to the key, your script needs to know where to send the request.

Look at the URL in your browser's address bar for the Supabase dashboard. You will see a project ID in it (tlfllnfwynaykxglsqhc).

This means your unique Supabase URL is: [https://tlfllnfwynaykxglsqhc.supabase.co](https://tlfllnfwynaykxglsqhc.supabase.co)

2. Create your .env File
We never want to hardcode API keys directly into pipeline.py because they could accidentally get pushed to GitHub. Instead, we use a hidden .env file.

Run this command in your Codespaces terminal to create the file at the root of your project:

Bash
touch .env
Open the .env file and paste in your URL and the Secret Key you copied:

Plaintext
SUPABASE_URL=
SUPABASE_KEY=